{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Fields — {{ module.label }}{% endblock %}

{#
	The three doors ([XIV-163]).

	This page used to be the whole editor: every field of every shape, every
	setting of every type, the order, the widths and the add form, in one table.
	It is now the question that comes before all of that, because the three
	things somebody comes here to do have almost nothing in common. Adding a
	field is answered by the *type* and then by that type's own options. Editing
	one starts by saying which. Arranging the form is the only one of the three
	that genuinely wants every field visible at once, and it is the only one that
	still looks like the old table.

	One set of doors per shape, because a module and its collections have
	separate fields (§5.1) and every door needs to know which shape it is about
	before it can ask anything else.
#}
{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between mb-3">
			<div>
				<h1 class="h3 mb-0">{{ 'common.fields'|trans }}</h1>
				<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">
					{{ 'field.intro'|trans({'%module%': module.label}) }}
				</p>
			</div>
			<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('module_index', {module: module.key}) }}">{{ 'field.back_to'|trans({'%module%': module.label|lower}) }}</a>
		</div>

		{#
			What this module has gained since it was installed (XIV-70).

			Here because this is where somebody comes when they want a field they
			have not got, and until now the only answer to "the new Contact has an
			addresses collection and mine does not" was to build it by hand, or, for
			a collection, to be told it cannot be done at all. A count and a link
			rather than the offer itself: taking one changes the shape of every
			record in the module, which is a page with a confirmation on the end of
			it and not a banner.

			Absent when there is nothing to take, which is the ordinary state of a
			module installed from this build. A permanent notice saying "nothing to
			do" is a notice people stop reading.
		#}
		{% if additions > 0 %}
			<div class="alert alert-info d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between" role="alert">
				<span>{{ 'upgrade.banner'|trans({'%count%': additions, '%module%': module.label}) }}</span>
				<a class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary" href="{{ path('module_upgrade', {module: module.key}) }}">{{ 'upgrade.banner_link'|trans }}</a>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			The first shape is the module itself and the rest are its collections
			(§5.1). Only a module has sections, which is why the third door says
			something slightly different on a collection: a collection's rows are
			drawn as a table on the record page, and a table row has nowhere to put
			a heading (XIV-119).
		#}
		{% for shape in shapes %}
			{% set unfinishedHere = shape.fields|filter(f => f.id in unfinished)|length %}
			<div class="card mb-3">
				<div class="card-body">
					{#
						The heading is an input, because the label is the customer's word
						and their module arrived named in whatever language it was
						installed in (XIV-8). It is here rather than behind a door because
						it is the one thing about a shape that is not about its fields at
						all, and there is nowhere else it belongs.
					#}
					<form method="post" action="{{ path('shape_rename', {module: module.key, shape: shape.id}) }}"
					      class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2 mb-3">
						<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('edit-fields') }}">
						<input class="form-control form-control-sm w-auto" name="label" value="{{ shape.label }}"
						       aria-label="{{ 'field.shape_name'|trans }}" required>
						<button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" type="submit">{{ 'common.rename'|trans }}</button>
						{% if not loop.first %}
							<span class="text-body-secondary small">{{ 'field.rows_inside'|trans({'%module%': module.label|lower}) }}</span>
						{% endif %}
					</form>

					<div class="row g-3">
						{#
							Door one. First on the row because it is the commonest act by
							far and it used to be the hardest to find: a form at the bottom
							of a table of everything somebody already has.
						#}
						<div class="col-md-4">
							<a class="card h-100 text-decoration-none link-body-emphasis"
							   href="{{ path('field_types', {module: module.key, shape: shape.id}) }}">
								<div class="card-body">
									<h2 class="h6 mb-1">{{ 'field.doors.add'|trans }}</h2>
									<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'field.doors.add_help'|trans }}</p>
								</div>
							</a>
						</div>
						{#
							Door two. The count is what makes it worth being a door rather
							than a link: it says whether there is one field to look through
							or forty.
						#}
						<div class="col-md-4">
							<a class="card h-100 text-decoration-none link-body-emphasis"
							   href="{{ path('field_list', {module: module.key, shape: shape.id}) }}">
								<div class="card-body">
									<h2 class="h6 mb-1">{{ 'field.doors.edit'|trans({'%count%': shape.fields|length}) }}</h2>
									<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'field.doors.edit_help'|trans }}</p>
									{#
										Drawn, saved, validated and useless (XIV-144): a choice field
										with no options accepts anything and offers nothing, a
										reference with no target names no record. Nothing added now
										can reach that state, so what this counts is a field older
										than the rule. Said out loud rather than left to be
										discovered, which is §8.3.1's whole argument.
									#}
									{% if unfinishedHere > 0 %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-warning mt-2">{{ 'field.doors.unfinished'|trans({'%count%': unfinishedHere}) }}</span>
									{% endif %}
								</div>
							</a>
						</div>
						{#
							Door three: the cross-field concerns. Everything behind it is
							decided by looking at the other fields, which is exactly why it
							is one page showing all of them.
						#}
						<div class="col-md-4">
							<a class="card h-100 text-decoration-none link-body-emphasis"
							   href="{{ path('field_arrange', {module: module.key, shape: shape.id}) }}">
								<div class="card-body">
									<h2 class="h6 mb-1">{{ 'field.doors.arrange'|trans }}</h2>
									<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">
										{{ loop.first ? 'field.doors.arrange_help'|trans : 'field.doors.arrange_help_rows'|trans }}
									</p>
								</div>
							</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
